 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the remaining tabs
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: after `closeTab`, the remaining tabs are renumbered `t1, t2, t3, …` in order and `next` is reset to
+  `tabs.length + 1`, so keys are always contiguous (closing `t2` of `t1,t2,t3` yields `t1,t2`).
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys. The maintainer explicitly named that decision and overrode it on 2026-10-23.
+- Consequence accepted: tabs to the right of a closed tab get new keys, so the React key changes and they remount
+  (scroll position and unsaved form input are lost — the 2026-08-29 incident behaviour).
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys contiguously", "open after close continues the contiguous numbering")
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §contiguous-tab-keys
 - Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+- Decision (no longer in force): a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
+- Was forbidden (now required): renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
+- Reason it was recorded: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
+  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29).
